---
// Not linked from the single-view homepage. It exists so search engines have
// something substantial to index about the company, and so anyone who lands
// here from a search result gets a page that stands on its own.
// Discovery is via /sitemap.xml and /robots.txt.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';

const year = new Date().getFullYear();

const facts = [
  { k: 'What we do', v: 'High-volume PDF generation and document pipelines' },
  { k: 'Run size', v: 'Thousands to several million documents' },
  { k: 'Platform', v: 'PodPDF — podpdf.com' },
  { k: 'Based in', v: 'Colombo, Sri Lanka' },
];
---

<Base
  title="About XAD Labs — document infrastructure for PDF at scale"
  description="XAD Labs is an independent engineering company in Colombo, Sri Lanka, building and operating the document infrastructure behind high-volume PDF generation — from thousands of documents to several million. Makers of PodPDF."
>
  <div class="flex min-h-screen flex-col">
    <header class="container-x shrink-0 border-b border-slate-200 py-5">
      <a href="/" class="inline-flex transition hover:opacity-80" aria-label="XAD Labs home">
        <Logo size="md" />
      </a>
    </header>

    <main class="container-x flex-1 py-16 sm:py-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-[2rem] font-normal leading-[1.12] text-slate-900 sm:text-[2.75rem]">
          An independent engineering company for documents at scale
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          XAD Labs builds and operates the document infrastructure behind high-volume PDF
          generation. We work at the end of the problem where general-purpose tools stop being the
          right answer — runs that start in the tens of thousands of documents and go into the
          millions, on pipelines built around one organisation's data and one organisation's rules.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="mt-12 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-200 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {
          facts.map((f) => (
            <div class="bg-white p-5">
              <dt class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-600">{f.k}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-[15px] font-semibold text-slate-900">{f.v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <div class="mt-16 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-16">
        <section>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.6rem] leading-tight text-slate-900">
            PDF generation at scale
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
            <p>
              Most document work is straightforward until the volume changes the problem. A script
              that converts a thousand files does not survive two million: retries, partial failure,
              cost per document and the length of the run itself all become part of the design
              rather than details to sort out later.
            </p>
            <p>
              We design the pipeline for that from the start. Statements, invoices, certificates and
              letters rendered from your own records, at run scale, with the same branding and
              layout on every page however long the run takes. Documents split out of enormous
              bundles, assembled into packets, read into structured data, or carried through a
              migration where every file has to be accounted for.
            </p>
            <p>
              The pipeline connects to the system the data already lives in — an ERP, a billing
              platform, a warehouse, object storage or an internal API — rather than waiting on
              someone to produce an export.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.6rem] leading-tight text-slate-900">
            And the reliability to match
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
            <p>
              Throughput is the easy half. The hard half is what happens when a run is four days
              long, part of the corpus fails, and the whole thing has to be answerable afterwards.
            </p>
            <p>
              Each document is its own unit of work, so re-running one changes nothing else and
              duplicates nothing. Work is persisted before it is attempted, and workers pull at the
              rate they can sustain, so a slow stage throttles instead of dropping. Runs are
              checkpointed: one that stops at 1.4 million documents resumes at 1.4 million, never
              from zero. Every file carries an audit row from ingest to delivery, so at the end of a
              run you can say what happened to any single document.
            </p>
            <p>
              Each engagement runs in its own environment, with its own credentials and its own
              retention window.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-16 max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.6rem] leading-tight text-slate-900">PodPDF, our platform</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          <p>
            <a
              href="https://podpdf.com"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="font-medium text-brand-600 underline decoration-brand-300 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-brand-500"
              >PodPDF</a
            > is our own product and the self-serve end of the same engine: a REST API and web app for
            generating, converting, merging and editing PDFs. HTML, Markdown, images and URLs in; a
            visual template editor for invoices, receipts, labels and certificates; bulk jobs,
            webhooks and a dashboard. Credit packs at $0.01 per document, with credits that never
            expire.
          </p>
          <p>
            We build it, run it and scale it ourselves, which is where most of what we know about
            PDFs at volume came from. When a project outgrows self-serve — a large archive, a run
            with a delivery date, documents that need handling rather than converting — that is the
            managed work described above.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-16 max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[1.6rem] leading-tight text-slate-900">Get in touch</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          A rough document count and a sentence about what needs to happen to them is enough to
          start. Write to
          <a href="mailto:zameer@xadlabs.com" class="font-medium text-brand-600 hover:underline"
            >zameer@xadlabs.com</a
          > and we will tell you quickly whether it is a project for us.
        </p>
        <a href="mailto:zameer@xadlabs.com" class="btn btn-primary mt-6">Start a conversation</a>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="container-x shrink-0 border-t border-slate-200 py-6">
      <div
        class="flex flex-col items-start justify-between gap-3 text-sm text-slate-500 sm:flex-row sm:items-center"
      >
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2">
          <a href="/" class="font-medium transition hover:text-brand-600">XAD Labs</a>
          <a
            href="https://podpdf.com"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="transition hover:text-brand-600">PodPDF ↗</a
          >
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/zameermfm/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="transition hover:text-brand-600">LinkedIn ↗</a
          >
        </div>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs">&copy; {year} XAD Labs</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </div>
</Base>
